-- A streamed turn to the ChatGPT backend (POST `CodexBase`/responses with `"stream":true`) goes over the backend's WebSocket, as Codex's own client sends it, and comes back as the server-sent events the HTTP route answers with (`codexSocket` in [codex_socket.go](../../internal/gateway/codex_socket.go), first in the gateway's `upstreamTransport`, so Codex's own sign-in passed through and magpie's ChatGPT accounts both take it, through the proxy in force). On some networks a streamed HTTP reply from chatgpt.com is cut at about 120 s, before `response.completed`, while the WebSocket runs to the end (brickhao on Discord). The handshake carries the turn's headers with `OpenAI-Beta: responses_websockets=2026-02-06`, without `x-codex-turn-state` and the responses-lite header, which go in the message's `client_metadata` as Codex puts them; the message is the body with `"type":"response.create"` ahead of it (a zstd body is decoded first). `codex.rate_limits` and `codex.response.metadata`, read ahead of the reply, are also told as the `x-codex-*` and `x-codex-turn-state` headers the HTTP route carries; `responsesapi.websocket_timing` is dropped. An `error` frame ahead of the reply is that status with its error as the body; `websocket_connection_limit_reached`, a refused handshake or a body that isn't a streamed turn goes over HTTP instead, and a host that refused the handshake (other than 401, 429 or 5xx) is spoken HTTP for 10 minutes (`codexSocketShun`). A socket that ends, or won't take the message, before any frame of the reply was handed on (any close status, a dropped connection, a normal close with no reply) sends the same body over HTTP (`codexSocketEnded`): Lamrfu's long conversation was closed 1009 (message too big) by chatgpt.com and got a 502. A conversation's socket is kept between its turns, and a turn that carries on from the last goes on it as only its new input after `previous_response_id`, as Codex's own client sends it (`codexKept` in [codex_socket_keep.go](../../internal/gateway/codex_socket_keep.go); codex-rs `get_incremental_items`): rem_thank on X saw magpie send the whole conversation on a new socket every turn, which a long one meets as 1009. A socket is kept for a turn that names its conversation (`session-id` or `thread-id`, else `prompt_cache_key`) and ended `response.completed` with an id, keyed by the host, a digest of `Authorization` and `chatgpt-account-id`, the conversation's ids and the subagent headers (`codexKeepKey`), so another account's turn never finds it nor names its response; the next turn takes it out of the pool, so two turns at once each have a socket. It is let go after 10 minutes unused (`codexKeepIdle`), at 55 minutes from its handshake (`codexKeepAge`; the backend ends a connection at 60) and the oldest past 32 (`codexKeepMax`); the gateway's tests drain the pool in their cleanup (`codexKeepDrain`). The turn goes as a delta when every field but `input`, `client_metadata` and `stream_options` is as the last turn sent it, and its input begins with the last turn's input and then that turn's `response.output_item.done` items, each compared without its id, status, nulls, empty lists, annotations and logprobs (`codexSocketItem`); anything else goes whole on the kept socket. What is compared is the body as it goes, after masking and every translation, so a turn masked otherwise than the last goes whole. A kept socket that fails before the reply is handed on (closed while kept, the write fails, or an `error` frame first, such as `previous_response_not_found` or the connection limit) is closed and the turn goes whole on a new socket, and from there over HTTP as before (`errCodexSocketAgain`). A message at least as big as one a host closed 1009 goes to it over HTTP for 10 minutes (`codexTooBig`). A socket closed after the reply began reads as a cut stream (`io.ErrUnexpectedEOF`), and is not asked again. Codex speaks to magpie over its own WebSocket since #4058 ([A Responses WebSocket](gateway-routing.md#a-responses-websocket)), sending a turn as a delta after `previous_response_id`; magpie gives each turn the whole conversation before it goes on, and this leg cuts its own delta for the kept socket. A turn on the socket tells the try's client trace of itself as Go's client tells it of an HTTP request (`socketTrace`): on a kept socket it is a request on an open connection, on a new one the handshake's DNS, connect and TLS stand and its round trip is the wait for the connection; the message written is the request written and the first frame the reply's first byte. So the Routing page's "Where the time went" (`TryNet`) and the usage record's split have the vendor's wait for every socket turn, where a kept socket's turn had nothing but its masking and a new socket's put the backend's wait in its first token (buhaodeshiyehao on X, `TestSocketTurnTellsWhereItsTimeWent`). The gateway's tests turn it off in TestMain (`codexSocketOff`); codex_socket_test's fakes speak it.
