 - A provider the user added may have its certificate left unchecked (`InsecureTLS`, the editor's Certificate row, #2383: a vLLM on the LAN, Ollama behind a self-signed cert, a company CA). Only one with a base URL of the user's own takes it (`TakesInsecureTLS`: no account, no preset, not Vertex, not a subscription's or plugin's id); `Save` refuses it on any other, and one written into providers.json by hand is ignored. `Provider.Via` then adds `netproxy.WithUncheckedTLS` with the hosts of its own base URLs (`UncheckedHosts`: Chat, Responses, Anthropic, Gemini, Decide), so every request made on its behalf through a `Dispatch` transport (the gateway's turns, count_tokens, models, test and detect, balance) skips the check for those hosts alone, on a transport kept apart for each (`route.unchecked`). A redirect or a models, balance or image URL on another host is checked, and `netproxy.With` drops the leave for a request made for another provider. An `https://` proxy would be dialled with the same TLS config, so its certificate would go unchecked too: such a request is refused (`ErrUncheckedOverHTTPSProxy`); an `http://` or `socks5://` proxy is taken. Backup, export and sync carry the field with the provider; an import link doesn't.
-- A provider the user added may show a client certificate and trust a CA of its own (`ClientCert`, `ClientKey`, `ServerCA`: absolute PEM paths, a `~` taken as home, the editor's Client certificate rows under Advanced, shown while an address is `https://`; #5596, clxsh: Remote Magpie to a magpie or an Nginx asking for mTLS). It takes the same providers as `InsecureTLS` (`TakesClientCert`), and `Save` refuses half a pair, a pair that doesn't match and a CA file with no certificate (`netproxy.CheckClientCert`). `Provider.Via` adds `netproxy.WithClientCert` with the hosts of its own base URLs, so the gateway's turns, streaming, models and the connection test show the certificate to those hosts alone, on a transport kept apart for each (`clientRoute`), the gateway key still sent; another host, and a request made for another provider, never see it. magpie's own requests of a Remote magpie take the same transport: its cards and quota history (`remoteBodyAs`), a key's limit (`askRemoteKeyLimit`), the model list's wait (`waitRemoteList`), and the Library's server for its MCP router, whose checks and calls go through `ViaRemoteMagpieAt` (`viaRemoteMagpie` in `internal/library/mcp_check.go`). A 403 from one is read as "isn't shared" only when the magpie there says so (`remoteRefused`); a client certificate check's or a proxy's refusal is said as it came (hhdaxia on X, `TestRemoteMagpieOwnRequestsShowClientCert`, `TestRemoteForbiddenSaysWhatRefused`, `TestRemoteMagpieRouterShowsClientCert`). Through an `https://` proxy such a request is refused (`ErrClientCertOverHTTPSProxy`). The route is keyed on the files' paths, mtimes and sizes, so a certificate renewed in place gets a new transport (`TestRemoteMagpieClientCert`, `TestClientCertSaveChecks`, the `clientcert_test.go` tests).
