 - The same providers may instead pin their server's certificate (`ServerPin`: the SHA-256 of its DER, lower-case hex; Zhangzhangi on X, a Remote magpie on a domain with a self-signed certificate macOS calls not standards compliant). Off by default, per provider. The editor's Advanced → Server certificate asks `POST /api/provider/peekcert`, which reaches the form's first `https://` address through its proxy and client certificate with the certificate unchecked (`Provider.PeekServerCert`) and shows its fingerprint, names, dates and why this computer doesn't take it (`x509.Verify` with the system roots); Trust keeps the pin, and the row then says in red that the certificate isn't verified. `Via` passes the pin to `netproxy.WithClientCert`, whose transport for those hosts skips the chain, name and date checks and refuses in `VerifyConnection` any leaf whose SHA-256 isn't the pin (`pinTLS`, `PinMismatchError`). The hosts it applies at are `PinHosts`: every endpoint of a provider that takes a client certificate, and of a preset's only the `https://` addresses at a host neither the preset nor its regions name (Zhangzhangi on X, "建议对别的自签名域名也生效": LM Studio or Ollama on another computer), never a subscription's, Vertex or a vendor's own host; a client certificate and Server CA still go only where `TakesClientCert` holds. `TakesServerPin` is in `/api/providers`, and the editor shows the row while the form has such an address (`pinAddrs` in app.js). `Save` refuses a pin that isn't 64 hex digits, or on a provider with no such address (`TestServerPinRemoteMagpie`, `TestServerPinSaveChecks`, `TestServerPinPresetAtOwnServer`, `TestServerPinNoVendorHost`, `TestPinOnlyThatCertificate`, `provider-server-pin.test.cjs`).
 - A provider may connect a host of its base URLs to addresses of the user's choosing, as `curl --resolve` (`Provider.Resolve`, a map of host name to places; quantum_lsfc on X, a managed network whose domains go to an internal L4 SNI gateway, where an IP in the base URL fails the certificate and `/etc/hosts` can't be edited). A place is an IP address (with `:port` if the gateway listens elsewhere) or `dns:<name>`, looked up at each new connection so a rotating record is followed. Only the TCP target changes: the URL's name is still the TLS server name and the `Host`. The places are tried in order, each given 8s (`resolveTry`); when none connects, the name is dialled as if there were no rule. `Via` puts the rule on the context (`netproxy.WithResolve`), `Dispatch` keeps one transport clone per rule and wraps its `DialContext` (`resolveTLS`). Through a proxy (the provider's, the global one, the environment's) the rule is not used: the proxy gets `CONNECT` with the original name and connects itself; `RoundTrip` marks a request direct (`directKey`) only when `t.Proxy` says none. `TakesResolve` is where `TakesInsecureTLS`, `TakesClientCert` or `PinHosts` hold: a provider the user added, Remote magpie, a preset at a host of the user's own; never a subscription's account or a vendor's own host. Plugins are not covered: a plugin's requests are `fetch` calls in the Bun plugin host, which has no hook for the connection's address, so a plugin's provider takes no rule (`TakesResolve` is false for an account). `Save` runs `netproxy.CheckResolve` (bare host names, IP or `dns:` places, at most 32 hosts and 16 places) and refuses a rule on a provider that doesn't take one. The gateway's offline check leaves such a provider's host out of the hosts it dials to tell whether the network is up (`otherHosts`), since it dials them by name. The editor's Advanced → Connect to is a box of `host = place, place` lines (`resolveRow` in tlscerts.js; `TestResolveSaved`, `TestResolveVia`, `TestOtherHostsSkipResolved`, `TestProviderSaveResolve`, the `netproxy` `TestResolve*` tests, `provider-resolve.test.cjs`).
 - A provider the user added may be held to HTTP/1.1 (`HTTP1`, the editor's "HTTP version" row under Advanced, shown while an address is `https://`; kepner_53726 on Discord: a company gateway whose HTTP/2 streams broke mid-reply with "tls: bad record MAC" while a plain Go client held). It takes the same providers as `InsecureTLS` (`TakesInsecureTLS`), and `Save` refuses it on any other. `Provider.Via` adds `netproxy.WithHTTP1` with the hosts of its own base URLs, so a request made on its behalf to one of those hosts goes through the `Dispatch` transport's HTTP/1.1 copy (`route.h1`); another host, and a request made for another provider (`netproxy.With` drops it), are offered HTTP/2 as before (`TestHTTP1AskedOnlyItsHost`, `TestHTTP1OwnProviderOnly`, `TestProviderSaveHTTP1`). Without it, a TLS record error read on an HTTP/2 reply (`recordError`: "bad record MAC", "received record with version …", an oversized record) moves that host:port to HTTP/1.1 for 6 hours (`h2BrokenFor`; see [Gateway routing](gateway-routing.md)).
-- A provider the user added with a Responses address may send its turns over the Responses WebSocket, as the ChatGPT leg above does (`Socket`, #5781, faithleysath; matrix0487 on Discord; `magpie provider set <id> socket=auto|on|off`, the editor's WebSocket row under Advanced). It takes a provider with no account, an `http(s)://` Responses address and not Vertex (`TakesSocket`); the CLI refuses it on any other. `Provider.Via` puts the provider's Responses host and path in the request's context (`withSocket`), and `codexSocket` speaks the socket only to a POST to that host at `<Responses>/responses` (`SocketTurn`); a provider without `Socket` hides a value an outer context carried, so it never dials a socket (`TestResponsesProviderWithoutSocketNeverDials`). Everything after is the ChatGPT leg's: the fallback to HTTP on a refused handshake or a socket that ends before the reply, the kept socket and its delta (keyed by scheme, `Authorization`, `X-Api-Key` and `Api-Key` too), and a server-sent-events reply. `auto` speaks HTTP to a host for 10 minutes once it refused the handshake; `on` tries the socket every turn and never shuns. Unset, a provider at `https://api.openai.com` (its Responses host exactly, no plugin) is `auto` (`SocketByDefault`, `SocketMode`), as Codex's built-in OpenAI provider speaks the socket there (codex-rs `create_openai_provider`, `supports_websockets: true`): Codex says `store: false`, so over HTTP every turn went up whole (KawaiiZapic on X, `TestOpenAIProviderTurnsGoOverWebSocketByDefault`); `off` is kept as set, so such a provider stays on HTTP, and the editor's tick opens ticked there, saving `off` unticked and unset ticked again. Before this an unticked tick saved unset, so a provider unticked then goes over the socket now until it is unticked again. Routing, keys, limits, usage and masking are the HTTP route's, since the socket replaces only the transport.
